"A system does one of two things: move the game forward, or enforce an invariant. Invariant enforcement is the more powerful mode: a system detects any invalid state within its concern and acts to make that state valid again, unconditionally, every tick. This removes the need to scatter bespoke triggers and timing across the codebase — you don't wire 'when X happens, do Y'; you write a system that guarantees the invariant regardless of how the state got invalid. Coordinate invariant systems purely by ORDERING them in the tick so each one's precondition is already established when it runs (one system's output is the next's detected condition). Examples: a party_leader pointing at a nonexistent entity is repointed at self; a room whose map instance no longer exists is cleaned up; a path whose destination no longer exists is deleted; an entity whose location no longer exists is relocated (player to checkpoint) or cleaned up (non-player)."